<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/advanced/entrepreneurship-and-venture-funding">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Steve Blank's definition is the load-bearing sentence of this lesson: a startup is a temporary organisation
    <strong>searching for a repeatable, scalable business model under extreme uncertainty</strong> — not a small
    version of a large company. That single distinction reorganises everything else. Searching is an
    experimental activity, so the right methods are experiments; the organisation is temporary, so the cap table
    and the funding ladder exist to buy time for the search; and extreme uncertainty means the base rates are
    brutal, so we will look at them honestly. The syllabus's business-activity and finance topics —
    classification, enterprise, forms of organisation, sources of finance, cash flow, the statements — all
    appear here, but arranged along the timeline of a venture from idea to term sheet.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="problem, loop, runway, terms" widget="equationStep" config={entCfg} />

  <h2>The enterprise: what a new business actually is</h2>
  <p>
    Business activity, at bottom, is <strong>adding value</strong>: buying or making something for less than
    customers will pay for it, in a form they want. Classification questions that look academic turn strategic
    fast: is the venture in the primary, secondary or tertiary sector (does it extract, make, or serve — and is
    it digital or physical, which changes marginal costs to near zero or not)? Is it private-sector for profit,
    a social enterprise, or a co-operative — a choice with real consequences for who the firm must satisfy?
    Most new businesses are sole traders or partnerships for a reason: unlimited liability is ugly, but
    formation is instant, control is total, and profits face no double layer. The move to a private limited
    company (Ltd in the UK, LLC or C-corp in the US) is not bureaucracy — it is the moment the firm can sell
    <em>shares</em>, which is the precondition for outside equity investment. A public listing is far beyond the
    startup stage, but the ladder matters: every funding round in this lesson presumes the corporate form that
    makes dilution possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Objectives deserve the syllabus's scepticism. Textbooks list survival, profit, growth and market share; the
    startup reality is that <strong>survival dominates</strong> for the first years, and the firm serves
    <em>stakeholders</em> with genuinely different objectives — customers want the product, employees want
    salaries and meaning, founders want control and wealth (Wasserman's data show these two pull apart), and
    investors want a return on a clock. Good founders name the trade-offs early instead of discovering them in
    a dispute.
  </p>

  <h2>The search method: lean, minimum, measurable</h2>
  <p>
    The lean loop — <strong>build, measure, learn</strong> — operationalises the search. Its unit of progress
    is <em>validated learning</em>: a fact about customers, demonstrated by their behaviour, that changes what
    you build next. The tool is the minimum viable product: the smallest artefact that generates real behaviour
    — a landing page with a pre-order button, a concierge service run by hand, a spreadsheet behind a polished
    face. The point of an MVP is to be <em>wrong cheaply</em>; every feature on it is a bet, and features are
    expensive. The discipline that separates lean from merely busy is the metric: choose one measurable
    hypothesis per cycle ("do 5% of visitors pre-order?"), run it, and accept the result. Eric Ries's deeper
    warning is about sunk cost — the startup's most dangerous asset is six months of work that the data say
    to abandon.
  </p>

  <h2>Funding: needs, sources and the runway clock</h2>
  <p>
    The finance-needs question is arithmetic first. Runway = cash ÷ net burn. With 600,000 in the bank and a
    50,000 monthly burn, runway is 12 months. Since institutional rounds take 3–6 months to close and founders
    should never negotiate with months to spare, the working rule is to <strong>start fundraising while at
    least 9 months of runway remain</strong> — a rule our example already violates at the margin. The
    <strong>sources</strong> ladder matches money to proof, cheapest first: personal savings and revenue
    (customer money is the cheapest capital that exists — it dilutes nothing); friends and family (fast,
    warm, socially risky); grants and competitions; angel investors (tens to hundreds of thousands, for a
    prototype with early users); seed funds; venture capital series A and beyond (millions, for demonstrated
    scaling); and venture debt, which buys months of runway without dilution but must be repaid regardless of
    success. Bootstrapping — growing on revenue alone — is not a consolation prize: it preserves 100% of the
    equity and forces pricing discipline, at the price of speed; plenty of durable companies (Basecamp is the
    canonical example) never raised a round. The honest statement: <strong>venture capital fits a narrow class
    of businesses</strong> — those that can grow extremely fast toward very large outcomes and where speed
    against competitors matters more than ownership.
  </p>

  <h2>The cap table: dilution by the numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Equity is the currency of this funding model, and it is spent in rounds. The arithmetic every founder must
    do on the spot: raising 500,000 at a <strong>5,000,000 pre-money</strong> valuation means a 5,500,000
    post-money, so the investor owns 500,000 / 5,500,000 ≈ <strong>9.1%</strong> — not 10%. Confusing pre- and
    post-money is the classic table error. Rounds compound: a founder at 40% before a 20% dilution holds 32%
    (40 × 0.8); three such rounds leave 20.5%. And every round typically enlarges the <strong>option
    pool</strong> — shares reserved for employees — which is often created post-money, meaning the founders
    absorb the dilution. Model the cap table three rounds forward before signing round one; founders who
    discover at series B that they hold 15% are discovering a fact that was fully visible in round one's
    paperwork.
  </p>

  <h2>Term sheets: valuation is the headline, terms are the economics</h2>
  <p>
    A term sheet prices the round and sets the rights. The clause that matters most is the
    <strong>liquidation preference</strong>: who gets paid first when the company sells. Worked example — an
    investor put in 10,000,000 for 25%, with a 1× <em>non-participating</em> preference; the company sells for
    30,000,000. The investor chooses the better of their 25% share (7,500,000) or their preference
    (10,000,000): they take 10,000,000 and the rest split 20,000,000. Now make the preference
    <em>participating</em>: they take 10,000,000 <strong>and then</strong> 25% of the remainder — 10,000,000 +
    7,500,000 × (25/75) = 12,500,000, leaving founders and employees 17,500,000. Stack a multiple (2×, 3×) on
    top and a "successful" exit can return investors everything while leaving common holders nothing — which
    is why sophisticated founders negotiate the preference stack harder than the valuation. Other standard
    clauses: board seats (who steers when interests diverge), pro-rata rights (investors may keep their
    percentage in later rounds), anti-dilution (protects earlier investors in a down round by repricing their
    shares), and vesting on founder shares (four years with a one-year cliff — protecting cofounders from each
    other when someone leaves early). The stages themselves are proofs, not prizes: pre-seed and seed capital
    buy evidence the product works; series A buys evidence it scales; B and beyond buy dominance in the market
    the earlier rounds discovered.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "raising at a higher valuation is winning." A high valuation is a loan
  against your next round's expectations: raise at 50 million, grow into 20 million of real progress, and the
  next round is a <em>down round</em> — brutal repricing, anti-dilution triggers, demoralised employees whose
  options are underwater. Raising slightly cheap at a valuation you can justify is the experienced move.</p>

  <h2>The pitch, decoded: what investors actually evaluate</h2>
  <p>
    A pitch deck is not a story about the product; it is an argument about a <em>return</em>, and its
    sections map to the investor's mental checklist. <strong>Market size</strong> is top-down sanity and
    bottom-up honesty: state the total addressable market (say 2 million small businesses in the segment),
    the slice you can reach (200,000 with your current channels), and the revenue you can build
    (200,000 × 30 a month = 72 million a year — bottom-up beats the famous "1% of a huge number" reflex,
    which investors read as a tell). <strong>Team</strong> is assessed as evidence, not biography: has this
    group shipped this kind of thing before, and does the founder-market fit explain why you? 
    <strong>Traction</strong> is the credibility engine — retention curves, weekly growth, letters of
    intent — because in power-law investing, evidence of engagement beats projection decks. 
    <strong>The moat question</strong> ("why do you win durably?") connects straight back to the strategy
    lesson. And the <strong>ask</strong> is arithmetic: "raising 750,000 for 18 months of runway to reach
    X users and a series-A-ready metric" — tying the number to milestones, because the investor's real
    question is whether this round buys proof that the next round prices higher. Rejection, read
    correctly, is market research about the return story, not the product's worth.
  </p>

  <h2>The runway arithmetic, extended</h2>
  <p>
    Because the runway rule does so much work, run it forward properly. Cash 600,000, burn 50,000 a month:
    12 months of runway. But burn is not constant — hiring pushes it up before revenue pushes it down, so
    the honest forecast is a burn <em>curve</em>: the same firm planning two sales hires sees month-6 burn
    at 65,000 and effective runway closer to 10 months. Revenue offsets burn (net burn = costs − revenue),
    which creates the growth-versus-runway dial every founder manages: cutting spend extends the runway but
    slows the traction that makes the next round possible. The professional instrument is the
    <strong>cash-out date</strong> computed monthly under three burn scenarios, and the discipline is
    deciding the raise threshold in advance — "we start conversations at 9 months" — while calm. Founders
    who compute it in month 3 raise on their terms; founders who compute it in month 11 raise on the
    investor's. The same arithmetic answers the bootstrapping comparison precisely: growing on revenue means
    the runway rule inverts — the product's sales <em>are</em> the funding line, slower, but with no dilution
    clock attached.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The base rates, stated honestly. The popular "90% of startups fail" is folklore; US Bureau of Labor
    Statistics data show about <strong>20% of new firms close within a year and roughly half within five</strong>
    — sobering, but far from coin-flip odds of instant death. Venture outcomes, however, are not normal:
    they follow a <strong>power law</strong>, where a small minority of investments return the whole fund and
    most return little. This has a counterintuitive consequence for founders: a VC rejecting you may not be
    judging your company as bad — they are judging it as not plausibly <em>huge</em>, because in power-law
    portfolios "pretty good returns" are the same as failure. It also explains the industry's systematic
    biases: investors overweight founders who resemble past outliers, and moderate-but-solid businesses —
    excellent companies by every ordinary measure — struggle to raise venture money not because they are weak
    but because they are the wrong shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wasserman's research programme on the <strong>founder's dilemmas</strong> quantifies the central trade-off:
    decisions that raise the odds of wealth (investors, experienced executives, broad cap tables) lower the
    odds of control, and vice versa; founders must choose which they are optimising for, because the data show
    you cannot reliably maximise both. And the highest-mortality risk is internal: cofounder conflict.
    The practical canon is to treat equity like employment — splits decided candidly, shares vesting over four
    years, roles and deadlocks documented while everyone still likes each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open questions: does venture funding <em>cause</em> success or merely <em>select</em> the ventures that
    would have succeeded (the identification problem that economics is still working through)? How will
    capital-efficient AI-era startups — four people, large revenue — change round structures built for
    headcount-heavy growth? And can the power law be tamed by ever-larger funds, or is it structural to
    innovation itself? Whatever the answers, the founder's discipline stands: search cheaply, measure honestly,
    protect the cap table, and read every term sheet as if the exit already happened.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em>; Blank, "Why the Lean Start-Up Changes Everything" (HBR 2013); Wasserman, <em>The Founder's Dilemmas</em>; US Bureau of Labor Statistics business employment dynamics.</p>

